    "description": "Mashhad's urban railway combines a segregated light-rail first line with later metro development. Airport access, interchange between lines, and a more direct connection to the Imam Reza shrine district are important elements of its documented expansion.",
    "tags": ["Mashhad", "Urban railway", "Light rail", "Metro", "Airport transport"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T12:51:09Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T12:51:09Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Mashhad Metro: Light Rail, Interchanges and Expansion",
      "description": "Explore the development of Mashhad's urban railway, from its first light-rail route to airport access and the initial Line 3 service."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "different-rail-formats",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "One system, different railway formats",
        "content": "Mashhad's urban railway illustrates why the word metro does not always describe identical infrastructure. UrbanRail.Net identifies Line 1 as fully segregated light rail using low-floor vehicles, with both underground and surface sections. It distinguishes Line 2 as a conventional metro with high-level platforms. Segregation means the first line has its own railway alignment; surface running alone does not make it a street tram. [s1]\n\nThe specialist chronology places the first line's trial service in 2011 and its airport extension in February 2016. Line 2's initial northeastern section opened in February 2017. Its southwestward extension reached Shariati in May 2018, creating interchange with Line 1. That connection changed the relationship between the routes: passengers could transfer between them instead of using two disconnected services. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "line-three",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Line 3: opening a section and improving its service",
        "content": "UrbanRail.Net records the start of Line 3 trial service in May 2025, serving Shohada, Bab Ol-Javad, and the Imam Reza coach terminal. These destinations explain the initial section's role: it links a coach terminal with the central shrine area and the existing railway network. [s1]\n\nA November 2025 Tasnim report provides a later operational milestone. Quoting municipal officials, it describes the opening of the second platform and operation with two trains, following an initial limited service. Officials reported shorter intervals and longer operating hours. These are attributed reports of service improvements at that date, rather than independently measured performance or a timetable guaranteed for subsequent years. [s2]\n\nThe Imam Reza shrine is a major Shia Muslim pilgrimage destination. In the report, municipal officials framed Line 3 as a way to improve access for both pilgrims and residents. Their claims that rail expansion would reduce congestion and improve environmental conditions express the project's intended benefits; the article does not supply an evaluation establishing those outcomes. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "completion-and-plans",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Distinguishing completed links from promises",
        "content": "The same November 2025 report treated Basij, the proposed interchange between Lines 1 and 3, as a future opening. UrbanRail.Net also listed its opening as scheduled rather than completed. Neither statement establishes that the transfer was available by this page's cutoff. [s1][s2]\n\nThe municipal account also discussed obtaining additional Line 1 vehicles and extending Line 3. Procurement steps and expected capacity increases should be kept separate from delivered vehicles and demonstrated passenger capacity. This distinction is particularly useful in Mashhad, where the inspected evidence shows that opening an initial section and strengthening its service were separate stages. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      }
    ],
